.TH LOGIN 6
.SH NAME
login \- key exchange protocol
.SH DESCRIPTION
A key exchange protocol is used between a client such as
.B login
in
.IR security-login (2),
and a certificate signing process such as
.IR logind (8),
to justify the latter's issuing a certificate that can
later be presented to an Inferno service
to establish credentials.
A shared secret must previously be agreed between
user and certifying authority (CA):
the CA holds either the 20-byte SHA-1 hash of the user's password
(see
.IR keyfs (4)),
which the user's side computes from the password,
or a
.I verifier
made from that hash, described below, which serves version 2 only.
.PP
There are two versions.
Version 2 is spoken unless version 1 is asked for;
neither side falls back from one to the other.
.SS "Version 2"
A secret made afresh for each run is used in CPace, a password-authenticated key exchange
(draft-irtf-cfrg-cpace, over X25519 with SHA-512),
run beside an ML-KEM-768 key exchange (FIPS 203).
The fresh secret is made in the manner of AuCPace, so that the CA need hold
nothing with which it, or someone who reads its files, could log in as the user.
Someone who does not know the secret, whether watching the exchange
or taking the part of user or CA, can test one guess at it for each run
and learns nothing else about it;
and a recording of a run is opened later only by breaking both X25519 and ML-KEM.
.PP
Until the last three lines, every message is a four-digit decimal count and a newline,
followed by that many bytes; an error is
.B !
and a three-digit count and a newline, followed by its text.
.IP
.EX
user→CA	2
CA→user	2
.sp 1v
user→CA	name, nonce-u[16], ML-KEM-768 encapsulation key[1184]
CA→user	nonce-c[16], X[32], Yc[32], ML-KEM-768 ciphertext[1088]
.sp 1v
user→CA	Yu[32], confirm-u[32]
CA→user	confirm-c[32]
.sp 1v
CA→user	CA's public key, alpha, p
user→CA	user's public key
CA→user	user's public key certificate
.EE
.PP
In CPace's terms the CA is the initiator and the user the responder.
The session identifier
.I sid
is the user's nonce followed by the CA's;
the channel identifier is
.B "inferno login 2"
and the name, each preceded by its length in a byte;
the secret (PRS) is the one described next;
the CA's associated data is the text
.B signer
and the user's is the name.
The generator is the Elligator 2 map of the first 32 bytes of the
SHA-512 hash of the draft's generator string;
.I Yc
and
.I Yu
are X25519 of each side's random 32 bytes with it;
and the CPace key
.I ISK
(64 bytes) is the draft's, from
.IR sid ,
the X25519 result (refused if it is all zeros) and both shares.
.PP
The password gives its user an X25519 secret key
.IR w :
32 bytes of HKDF-SHA256 with no salt, the SHA-1 hash of the password as the input,
and as the info the text
.BR "inferno login 2 verifier" ,
a zero byte, and the name.
Its public key
.I W
is the user's verifier.
The CA has
.I W
(it stores it, as the 8 bytes
.B aucpace1
followed by
.IR W ;
or it stores the SHA-1 hash and computes
.I W
when asked).
For each run the CA makes a fresh X25519 key pair and sends the public key
.IR X .
PRS is the X25519 value both can then compute:
the user from
.I w
and
.IR X ,
the CA from
.I W
and the secret key of
.IR X ;
an all-zero value is refused.
To log in one must know
.IR w ,
which
.I W
does not give; a CA that stores only
.I W
can check a password, and someone who steals
.I W
can test guesses at the password against it, but neither can
take the user's part.
A CA that stores the SHA-1 hash has in it everything the user's side
makes of the password.
.PP
This is the form of version 2 that has
.IR X ;
an earlier draft without it was never issued.
.PP
.I T
is the SHA-256 hash of
.B "inferno login 2"
and a zero byte, followed by each message from the name to
.IR Yu ,
in the order above, each preceded by a byte
.B c
(from the user) or
.B s
(from the CA) and its length as four bytes, most significant first.
The key material is the 32-byte ML-KEM secret followed by
.IR ISK .
Each key is HKDF-SHA256 with
.I T
as the salt, that material as the input, 32 bytes of output, and one of these as the info:
.IP
.EX
inferno login 2 confirm c
inferno login 2 confirm s
inferno login 2 c2s
inferno login 2 s2c
.EE
.PP
.I Confirm-u
is the first and
.I confirm-c
the second.
The CA checks the user's confirmation before sending anything more;
if it is wrong it answers with the error
.RB `` "name or secret incorrect" ''.
It answers a name it does not know, or one whose entry has expired,
in the same way and at the same point, having run the exchange with a verifier nobody has the key to.
The last three lines travel in
.B chacha20poly1305
records of
.IR ssl (3),
keyed by the last two keys (from user to CA, and from CA to user),
in the format of
.B getstring
in
.IR keyring-getstring (2).
.I Alpha
and
.I p
are the parameters version 1 of
.IR auth (6)
uses.
The user checks that the certificate is the CA's signature of the key it sent.
.SS "Version 1"
The original protocol is an encrypted key exchange, spoken through
.IR ssl (3)
from the first byte.
A client uses it only when told to
.RB ( "getauthinfo -1" ,
or an address that starts
.B "v1 "
given to
.BR login ),
and
.I logind
serves it only when given
.BR -1 .
A version 2 client that meets a CA with only version 1 gets no answer and gives up after 20 seconds.
Version 1 needs the SHA-1 hash at the CA: a user whose entry is a verifier cannot use it.
.PP
A shared secret must previously be agreed between
user and certifying authority (CA).
It is used by the protocol to establish a secure channel between user and CA.
.PP
In the description below:
.TF key(m)
.PD
.TP
.I ivec
is an 8 byte random number (`initialisation vector') chosen for
this conversation.
.TP
.I sha
is the 20 byte secure hash (SHA-1) of the password
.TP
.I key
is an 8 byte secret formed as follows:
.EX
.br
key[0] = ivec[0]^sha[0]^sha[8]^sha[16]
key[1] = ivec[1]^sha[1]^sha[9]^sha[17]
.EE
\&...
.EX
key[5] = ivec[5]^sha[5]^sha[13];
key[6] = ivec[6]^sha[6]^sha[14];
key[7] = ivec[7]^sha[7]^sha[15];
.EE
.TP
.I alpha
is a Diffie-Hellman base used system wide
.TP
.I p
is a Diffie-Hellman modulus used system wide
.TP
.I "key(m)"
is
.I m
encrypted using the RC4 algorithm with
.IR key .
.TP
.I Rx
is a random number of the same order as
.IR p .
.TP
.I "secret"
is the Diffie-Hellman secret
.IR "alpha**(r0*r1) mod p" .
.PP
The protocol follows.  ``user→CA xxx'' means that the user
sends the message ``xxx'' to the certifying authority.
Any party can send an error instead of a message at any
point to terminate the protocol.
.IP
.EX
user→CA	name
CA→user	ACK
.sp 1v
user→CA	ivec
CA→user	key(alpha**r0 mod p), alpha, p
.sp 1v
user→CA	alpha**r1 mod p
CA→user	CA's public key, SHA(CA's public key + secret)
.sp 1v
user→CA	user's public key, SHA(user's public key + secret)
CA→user	user's public key certificate
.EE
.PP
The complexity of this protocol is intended to shield the password.
To start a clear text attack against the password, one
needs to first attack the Diffie-Hellman exponential
to determine
.IR "alpha**r0 mod p" .
A possible weakness is that the encrypted quantity
is base64 encoded, constraining
the possible values of each byte.
This could aid a brute force attack.
.PP
.I Alpha
and
.I p
are sent unprotected, though the user code does a few sanity checks
on the values it receives.
This is another likely point of attack.
We should like to know about any.
.PP
The role of
.I ivec
is to foil any replay attacks by someone spoofing the CA
though this is probably overkill.
.PP
The key that hides
.I "alpha**r0 mod p"
is used by RC4 as a 40-bit key, and what it hides has a known form,
so one recorded run allows the 40 bits to be found by trial;
with them an attacker can pose as the user to the CA.
That is why version 2 exists.
.SH SEE ALSO
